quilibrium allocation (x*, y*) there must exist a vector of Pareto weights \u03bb such that (x*, y*) maximizes the \u03bb-weighted Welfare function (as we saw in the Second welfare theorem notes).\n- Negishi's method relies on this fact, and instead of trying to compute the equilibrium prices for the economy, it tries to find the corresponding vector of Pareto weights.\n- Once the right vector of Pareto Weights is found, one can find the Walrasian equilibrium allocation, and we can use the technique developed in the Second Welfare theorem to derive the equilibrium prices from the Pareto Optimal allocation."]}, "id": "BB_2", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 17.185171385991058, "y": 96.72131147540983, "width": 15.676229508196723, "height": 2.533532041728762, "rotation": 0}, "id": "zzlcsZRINJ", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "manual"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 17.185171385991058, "y": 96.72131147540983, "width": 15.676229508196723, "height": 2.533532041728762, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "zzlcsZRINJ", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "manual"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 17.185171385991058, "y": 96.72131147540983, "width": 15.676229508196723, "height": 2.533532041728762, "rotation": 0, "text": ["Robert M. Townsend (MIT)"]}, "id": "zzlcsZRINJ", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "manual"}], "created_username": "10. NAIST nlp_annot_10@naist.ac.jp, 16", "created_ago": "0\u00a0minutes", "completed_by": {"id": 16, "first_name": "10. NAIST", "last_name": "", "email": "nlp_annot_10@naist.ac.jp"}, "task": {"id": 137995, "data": {"ocr": "/lectrans/data/local-files/?d=/large/datas/